# Hand-written type seed fed to Spinel via `--rbs` (see spin build and the CI
# golden check). Unlike test/git-stack.rbs.expected -- which is the *emitted*
# golden and must never live under rbs/, or it would seed itself -- this file is
# an input: signatures we assert so the analyzer keeps these methods on the
# concrete fast path.
#
# `effective_parent` (bin/git-stack.rb) unifies these methods' branch-name
# parameters with its own result and, left to inference alone, widens them to
# Spinel's untyped slow path. Each takes a branch (or parent) name -- always a
# String -- so pinning them here is a faithful assertion, not a fib to the
# compiler; it keeps the compiled binary unboxed and the emitted golden free of
# new untyped.
#
# `effective_parent` is pinned for the same reason one step out: it takes the
# trunk LIST (it answers "is this branch itself a trunk?" before resolving the
# parent), and an `Array[String]` parameter inferred from scratch widens to
# `Array[untyped]`, which then flows out through what its result feeds --
# `branch_ref_exists?` and `checkout!` in `cmd_down`.
#
# `containing_trunk` and `restack_subtree` are the trunk-list pair: the first
# picks the trunk a branch rests on, so its untyped result would spread through
# every reparent it feeds (`set_parent`, `record_reparent_base`, `reparent!`);
# the second now takes the whole list rather than one caller-chosen trunk, and
# widens the same way `effective_parent` does. Both are faithful: a branch name
# and the trunk list, String and Array[String] at every call site.
#
# StackTopology is built from an already-read config scan and the current ref
# set. StackSnapshot owns the Git-reading factory and the history-derived
# ahead/behind index, keeping the topology itself pure.
#
# Everything below the first four entries was added when Spinel moved to
# 0b8527df4b87, whose inference propagated that same unification further than
# the pin before it: without them the golden carried widened `class Object`
# signatures (`paint`, `green`, `set_trunks`, `is_trunk?`, `clear_parent`,
# `clear_base`, `untrack!`) that had been concrete.
#
# Under the Spinel pinned now (ci.yml holds the ref; this file deliberately does
# not repeat it) the METHOD pins remain redundant -- inference holds those
# signatures on its own, and the golden is byte-identical with them removed. They stay anyway, and that is deliberate: a pin is cheap,
# and being redundant against TODAY'S compiler is not the same as being
# unnecessary. It is what stops the next bump from quietly widening a signature
# instead of failing the golden diff. This file has already learned that once --
# `rbs/paint.rbs` was deleted on exactly the "inference no longer needs it"
# reasoning, and the `paint`/`green` pins had to be written again one bump later.
#
# The StackTopology / StackSnapshot ivar pins at the bottom are the working exception: they are
# NOT redundant today. They are what took the golden from 41 untyped to 39, and
# what made the bump to master tip safe -- without them the same source emits 49
# there, and 40 with only `@ab` dropped (see the ci.yml note that measures each
# candidate ref).
#
# `parent_note` and `untracked_parent?` are the issue #85 pair: the note `tree`
# prints is now also read by `parent`/`down`, so its branch-name parameter is
# unified across three call sites instead of one. That is exactly the shape the
# first four entries were written for, and both assertions are faithful -- a
# branch name and the topology it is asked about, String and StackTopology at
# every call site.
#
# `named_children_of` joins `children_of` as the fix-wave's own pair: `up
# <name>` now reads a second one-shot topology alongside the menu's, built the
# same way and returned the same shape, so it is unified right along with it.
# Faithful for the same reason `children_of` is -- a parent name and the trunk
# list, String and Array[String].
#
# `live_trunks` / `trunk_branches` are pinned against a CRASH rather than a
# golden diff -- the one failure mode this file's rule does not cover. Widen
# `live_trunks`'s `select` and `trunk_branches` answers a poly array on the
# configured path and the `[trunk]` literal's string array on the detect path;
# callers unbox both with the same unchecked cast, so the first `trunk?`
# segfaults on any repo with `stack.trunk` set rather than surfacing as a new
# `untyped` line (17447ac has the codegen). `live_trunks` is the entry doing
# that work -- pinned, both of `trunk_branches`' return paths are `Array[String]`
# already, so its own entry is this file's cheap-pin rule, not a second
# assertion. Both faithful: the trunk list, `Array[String]` at every call site.
#
# `cyan` through `replay_onto!` are the next wave of that same unification, and
# they are the 0b8527df story a second time: eleven `class Object` signatures
# that had been concrete widened at their branch-name (and packed-list)
# parameters, taking the golden from 39 untyped to 54 in one bump. Every entry
# restates the signature the previous ref inferred unaided, so the emitted
# golden is what proves each faithful before it is asserted here. `cyan` is the
# `rbs/paint.rbs` lesson arriving again, on the one colour helper the earlier
# wave left unpinned.
#
# `Set#merge` at the bottom is the same assertion one class out: our only two
# calls pass `order_branches`'s `Array[String]`, and the element type is lost
# on the way in without it.
#
# `branch_ref` is the one entry here that was NOT written after a golden diff:
# it is a new method (issue #96), and it is the exact shape this file's first
# paragraph describes -- a branch-name parameter reached from a dozen call sites,
# `effective_parent`'s unification among them. Unpinned it emits `(untyped) ->
# String` and takes the golden to 43, one over RATCHET; pinned it is concrete and
# the count holds at 42. Faithful for the usual reason: every caller passes a
# branch name, String at all of them.
#
# `ahead_behind_columns` / `ahead_behind_readback` widened in that same wave and
# are deliberately NOT pinned, which is this file's one worked example of an
# unfaithful assertion. Their `Hash[String, Integer]` is what the old ref
# inferred, but the new one builds a poly hash there on purpose (spinel 328a5ac:
# an empty-hash local keyed by a block result cannot be assumed String-keyed,
# because the String-keyed variant reads a boxed key as a `const char *`).
# Asserting the old signature does not narrow that hash, it reinterprets it --
# `cc` says so, `incompatible pointer types ... 'sp_PolyPolyHash *' from a
# function with result type 'sp_StrIntHash *'` -- and `tree` segfaults on the
# fixture past the 4 KB scan boundary. The golden proving a signature is
# necessary but not sufficient: it proves the OLD compiler inferred it, not that
# the new one still represents it that way. Untyped and correct beats concrete
# and reinterpreted, so these two stay on the slow path.
#
# So: add a pin when a widening makes one necessary, and keep it once the need
# passes -- but only where the assertion is still true of how the value is
# actually built. Every entry here is a faithful assertion -- branch names,
# colour text, and the trunk list, all String / Array[String] at every call site
# -- so none of them can lie to the compiler, whatever inference does next.
class Object
  def sh: (String) -> String
  def branch_ref: (String) -> String
  def checkout!: (String) -> nil
  def branch_name_taken?: (String) -> bool
  def branch_ref_exists?: (String) -> bool
  def ahead_behind: (String, String) -> Array[Integer]
  def paint: (String, String) -> String
  def green: (String) -> String
  def cyan: (String) -> String
  def set_trunks: (Array[String]) -> Array[String]
  def live_trunks: (Array[String]) -> Array[String]
  def trunk_branches: () -> Array[String]
  def is_trunk?: (String, Array[String]) -> bool
  def containing_trunk: (String, Array[String]) -> String
  def effective_parent: (String, Array[String]) -> String
  def set_parent: (String, String) -> bool
  def clear_parent: (String) -> bool
  def get_base: (String) -> String
  def set_base: (String, String) -> bool
  def clear_base: (String) -> bool
  def record_tip_base: (String, String) -> nil
  def untrack!: (String) -> nil
  def restack_subtree: (String, Array[String], bool, String, StackTopology) -> nil
  def children_of: (String, Array[String]) -> Array[String]
  def named_children_of: (String, Array[String]) -> Array[String]
  def tree_marker: (String, String) -> String
  def tree_name: (String, String, String) -> String
  def pack_lines: (Array[String]) -> String
  def parent_note: (String, StackTopology) -> String
  def print_parent_note: (String, Array[String]) -> nil
  def print_tree_row: (String, Integer, String, StackSnapshot) -> nil
  def resolve_stack_base: (String, String) -> String
  def replay_onto!: (String, String, String) -> nil
end

# `parent_of` / `children_of` join `branch?` for the same reason, and the graph
# queries `stack_root` / `would_cycle?` / `validate_new_parent!` join them --
# becoming methods on the topology relocated their pins here rather than retiring
# them. They used to take the trunk LIST besides, which widened exactly as
# `effective_parent`'s does; the topology now keeps its trunks in a Set and
# answers `trunk?` itself, so the parameter (always the same list the topology
# was built from) is gone and only the branch names are left to pin.
#
# The three ivar pins below are the seed's other half, and they are what keeps
# this file's method pins from being fought uphill: `@parents` and `@children`
# had drifted to `Hash[String, untyped]` / `Hash[untyped, untyped]` on their
# own, and every reader of a widened `@children` widens with it -- `order`,
# `order_branches`, `walk_order`, `detached_root(s)`, and `print_order` /
# `cmd_tree` one call further out. Pinning the fields is one assertion each
# instead of eight, and it holds them at the source.
#
# They are faithful: StackTopology's `@parents` maps a branch name to its
# recorded parent and `@children` a parent to its newline-PACKED child rows.
# StackSnapshot owns `@ab`, mapping a branch to packed "<behind>\t<ahead>".
#
# The packing is what makes those String -> String pins expressible at all, and
# it is worth knowing WHY the obvious alternative is not a pin you can write:
# `@children: Hash[String, Array[String]]` over an array-valued hash is dropped
# on the floor -- not applied, not refused -- and the field still emits as
# `Hash[String, untyped]`. A seed line that has no effect is worse than none,
# because it reads like a guarantee. See pack_lines in bin/git-stack.rb.
#
# Pinning them also puts `children_of` / `walk_order` back where their comments
# assume they are: with `@children` concrete, their `.to_s` guards before
# `.split` are no-ops again (measured: removing both leaves the golden
# unchanged) rather than the load-bearing re-narrowing they had become. The
# guards stay anyway, on this file's standing rule -- a pin is cheap, and being
# redundant against today's compiler is not the same as being unnecessary.
#
# `detached_roots` joins them for the third piece of the same change: `up` now
# reads it, so its hand-built `[]` is unified with the concrete children list it
# is appended to, and an `Array[untyped]` there would take `cmd_up`'s `include?`
# / `length` / `[0]` down with it.
class StackTopology
  @parents: Hash[String, String]
  @children: Hash[String, String]
  def branch?: (String) -> bool
  def parent_of: (String) -> String
  def children_of: (String) -> Array[String]
  def detached_roots: () -> Array[String]
  def live_detached_roots: () -> Array[String]
  def tracked_branches: () -> Array[String]
  def parent_missing?: (String) -> bool
  def untracked_parent?: (String) -> bool
  def reconnect_target: (String, String) -> String
  def stack_root: (String) -> String
  def would_cycle?: (String, String) -> bool
  def validate_new_parent!: (String, String, String) -> nil
  def load: (String) -> nil
  def self.from_scan: (Set, Array[String], String) -> StackTopology
end

class StackSnapshot
  @topology: StackTopology
  @ab: Hash[String, String]
  def topology: () -> StackTopology
  def ahead_behind_of: (String) -> Array[Integer]
end

class StackRepository
  def self.load_topology: (Array[String]) -> StackTopology
  def self.load_snapshot: (Array[String]) -> StackSnapshot
end

class Set
  def merge: (Array[String]) -> Set
end
